---
import BaseLayout from '~/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Figure from '~/components/Figure.astro';
import Assumptions from '~/components/Assumptions.astro';
import Callout from '~/components/Callout.astro';
import Math from '~/components/Math.astro';
import Badge from '~/components/Badge.astro';
import Cite from '~/components/Cite.astro';
import QEqLab from '~/islands/QEqLab';

const assumptions = [
  { term: 'Parameters', value: 'Toy electronegativities χ and hardnesses J in eV (H 4.5/13.9, C 5.3/10.1, O 8.7/13.4, Na 2.8/7.0, Cl 8.6/11.0), ordered like real values but not fitted. The χ slider shifts one atom’s χ.' },
  { term: 'Kernel', value: 'Shielded Coulomb γᵢⱼ = k/∛(rᵢⱼ³ + a³), a = 1.6 Å, k = 14.3996 eV·Å, so the interaction stays finite at short range. Chosen so that typical charges stay below one electron.' },
  { term: 'Geometry', value: 'Water and ethanol in their G2 geometries (ASE); the Na–Cl pair on one axis. Positions are fixed: only charges respond.' },
  { term: 'Units & signs', value: 'Charges in e (positive = electron-poor), energies and μ in eV. Total charge is held fixed exactly.' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Charge equilibration"
  description="Physics Lab P8: partial charges from electronegativity equalisation — charge flows until every atom has the same effective electronegativity — and the textbook failure of the method when two atoms are pulled apart."
>
  <article class="page-grid lab-page">
    <header class="wide head">
      <p class="eyebrow"><a href="/physics-lab/">Physics Lab</a> · P8</p>
      <h1 class="h-display">Charge equilibration</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Long-range models need charges, and charges are not fixed: they respond to the environment. Charge equilibration
        lets them flow like water finding its level, until no atom can lower the energy by passing charge to another.
      </p>
      <div class="head-badges">
        <Badge label="toy-model" />
        <Badge label="theory" />
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="block" id="derivation" aria-labelledby="eq-h">
      <h2 id="eq-h" class="h-section">Equal electronegativity</h2>
      <p>
        Give each atom an electronegativity χ (how much it wants charge) and a hardness <i>J</i> (how much it resists
        holding it), and let charges interact through a shielded Coulomb kernel γ. The energy of a set of charges is
      </p>
      <Math display number="1" tex={String.raw`E(\mathbf q) = \sum_i \Big(\chi_i q_i + \tfrac12 J_i q_i^2\Big) + \sum_{i<j} \gamma_{ij}\, q_i q_j, \qquad \sum_i q_i = Q`} />
      <p>Minimising at fixed total charge with a Lagrange multiplier μ gives one linear equation per atom:</p>
      <Math display number="2" tex={String.raw`\frac{\partial E}{\partial q_i} = \chi_i + J_i q_i + \sum_{j\neq i}\gamma_{ij}\,q_j = \mu \quad\text{for every } i`} />
      <p>
        Every atom ends with the same effective electronegativity μ — the electronegativity-equalisation principle at the
        heart of QEq <Cite id="rappe1991qeq" />.
      </p>
    </section>

    <div class="wide fig-wrap">
      <Figure title="Charges flowing to equalise electronegativity" labels={['toy-model', 'theory']} number={1} id="fig-qeq" framed={false}>
        <QEqLab client:visible />
        <Fragment slot="caption">
          Atoms are coloured by their equilibrated charge (blue positive, amber negative). Top right: the charges. Bottom
          right: each atom’s electronegativity before charge flows (grey) and after (green), which all land on μ. For the
          Na–Cl pair the bottom panel shows the charge on Cl as the ions separate.
        </Fragment>
        <Assumptions slot="assumptions" items={assumptions} />
        <Fragment slot="source">Method: <Cite id="rappe1991qeq" />. Solved exactly in your browser (one linear system per change).</Fragment>
      </Figure>
    </div>

    <section class="block" id="dissociation" aria-labelledby="diss-h">
      <h2 id="diss-h" class="h-section">The dissociation problem</h2>
      <p>
        Take a neutral Na–Cl pair and pull the atoms apart. The Coulomb kernel vanishes, γ → 0, and equation (2) for the
        two atoms with <i>q</i><sub>Cl</sub> = −<i>q</i><sub>Na</sub> becomes
      </p>
      <Math display number="3" tex={String.raw`\chi_{\mathrm{Na}} + J_{\mathrm{Na}}\,q = \chi_{\mathrm{Cl}} - J_{\mathrm{Cl}}\,q \;\Longrightarrow\; q_{\mathrm{Na}} = \frac{\chi_{\mathrm{Cl}} - \chi_{\mathrm{Na}}}{J_{\mathrm{Na}} + J_{\mathrm{Cl}}} \neq 0`} />
      <p>
        Two atoms a kilometre apart still share a fraction of an electron. Real atoms separate neutral. Equation (3) is
        an exact consequence of the model, not a numerical artefact: the cost of moving charge, set by χ and <i>J</i>, does
        not grow with distance.
      </p>
      <Callout kind="caveat" title="Why this matters for MLIPs">
        Long-range MLIPs that use charge equilibration typically predict χ (and sometimes <i>J</i>) from each atom’s
        environment with a neural network, which changes the parameters but not the structure of equations (1)–(3).
        How a given model handles charge transfer across long distances is a property to check for that model, not an
        assumption to make.
      </Callout>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .lab-page {
    padding-block: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 4rem) clamp(3rem, 6vw, 5rem);
  }
  .head .eyebrow a {
    color: inherit;
    text-decoration: none;
  }
  .head h1 {
    margin-block: 0.6rem 1rem;
    font-size: clamp(2.1rem, 4.4vw, 3.6rem);
    line-height: 1.05;
  }
  .lede {
    font: 1.1875rem/1.6 var(--font-serif);
    color: var(--atlas-ink-muted);
    max-width: 44rem;
  }
  .head-badges {
    display: flex;
    gap: 0.35rem;
    margin-top: 1rem;
  }
  .block {
    margin-top: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 3rem);
    font: 1.0625rem/1.7 var(--font-serif);
    color: var(--atlas-ink);
  }
  .block p {
    margin-block: 0 1rem;
  }
  .block a {
    color: var(--atlas-arch);
  }
  .fig-wrap {
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }
</style>
